.TH "RK\-VIM\-RECOVER Reference" "" "" ""
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]vim \-r\f[R]: Launch Vim showing where swap files are located
(e.g., have an open instance of Vim with unsaved changes, then run
\f[CR]vim \-r\f[R] in the same directory to list the swap files)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]:recover\f[R]: Recover the current file from a swap file
.SH Menu
If a \f[CR].swp\f[R] file exists for the file being edited, a menu will
be presented with options.
\f[I]The \f[CI](D)elete\f[I] option will only be available if the
process that created the \f[CI].swp\f[I] file isn\(cqt running.\f[R] So
if delete is not present then another instance of Vim is probably
editing this file.
.SH Differences
To view the differences between the \f[CR].swp\f[R] file and the current
file.
.IP "1." 3
Edit the file and recover the \f[CR].swp\f[R], after this the buffer
will be the \f[CR].swp\f[R] contents, but the current file hasn\(cqt
changed yet
.IP "2." 3
Run \f[CR]:w !diff % \-\f[R] to see a diff of the buffer contents versus
the unchanged file on disk
.IP "3." 3
Changes will be shown with the current file prefixed with \f[CR]<\f[R]
and changes from the buffer (\f[CR].swp\f[R]) will be shown with
prefixed with \f[CR]>\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
After saving or discarding changes, \f[CR]:e\f[R] will get back the menu
again where the option to delete the \f[CR].swp\f[R] file can be chosen
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
< Current file
\-\-\-
> Swamp file
.EE
